Recursively enumerable and recursively decidable predicates on the natural numbers, together with the primitive-recursive coding of lists used to present neighbourhoods.

<!-- formalization -->

**Theorem (`amGM`).** For natural numbers, four times their product is at most the square of their sum.

*Proof.* Expanding the square reduces the inequality to nonnegativity of the square of the difference of the two numbers. ∎

**Theorem (`iter_sq_le`).** Every value returned by the square-root iteration has square at most the input.

*Proof.* Induction over the square-root iteration uses the arithmetic–geometric mean inequality to show that each positive update remains below the input after squaring; the zero branch is immediate. ∎

**Theorem (`lt_of_mul_lt_mul_left'`).** A strict inequality after multiplication by a common natural factor implies the corresponding strict inequality of the other factors.

*Proof.* A strict product inequality forces the common factor to be positive; cancellation of that positive factor then gives the strict inequality between the remaining factors. ∎

**Theorem (`lt_iter_succ_sq`).** If the input lies below the square of one more than an initial guess, it also lies below the square of one more than the resulting square-root iterate.

*Proof.* Induction on the iteration compares the arithmetic-mean update with the preceding guess; the arithmetic–geometric mean estimate and cancellation of a positive factor preserve the strict upper-square bound. ∎

**Theorem (`lt_succ_sqrt`).** Every natural number is strictly below the square of one more than its square root.

*Proof.* The square root satisfies the lower-square bound, and the next consecutive square is therefore strictly above the input by the interval characterization. ∎

**Theorem (`sqrt_le`).** The square of the natural square root of a number is at most that number.

*Proof.* The square-root iteration already has square at most the input, and the natural square root is the iteration begun from the input itself. ∎

**Theorem (`sqrt_eq_of`).** If a number lies between two consecutive squares beginning at the square of a given integer, its natural square root is that integer.

*Proof.* The two hypotheses place the number in the unique interval from one square up to the next consecutive square. ∎

**Theorem (`sqrt_add_eq`).** Adding at most twice an integer to its square does not change its natural square root.

*Proof.* The hypothesis places the added term below twice the base, so the result lies from the base square up to, but not including, the next square; the square-root interval characterization applies. ∎

**Theorem (`unpair_pair`).** Unpairing a paired pair of natural numbers recovers the original ordered pair.

*Proof.* Substitution of the explicit pairing formula identifies its square-root block; the resulting quotient and offset calculations recover both entries. ∎

**Theorem (`unpair_pair_fst`).** The first component obtained by unpairing a paired pair is its first entry.

*Proof.* Taking first components in the pairing inverse identity recovers the first entry. ∎

**Theorem (`unpair_pair_snd`).** The second component obtained by unpairing a paired pair is its second entry.

*Proof.* Taking second components in the pairing inverse identity recovers the second entry. ∎

**Definition (`selectFn`).** This selector returns its first value when its numerical control is one and its second value when the control is zero.

**Theorem (`selectFn_one`).** A selector controlled by one returns its first value.

*Proof.* Substitution of control one cancels the second summand and leaves the first value. ∎

**Theorem (`selectFn_zero`).** A selector controlled by zero returns its second value.

*Proof.* Substitution of control zero cancels the first summand and leaves the second value. ∎

**Theorem (`selectFn_ite`).** A zero-one control obtained from a proposition makes numerical selection agree with ordinary case distinction.

*Proof.* In the true case the Boolean control is one and in the false case it is zero; the two selector equations settle the cases. ∎

**Definition (`RecDecidable`).** A predicate on natural numbers is recursively decidable when a primitive-recursive characteristic function takes the value one exactly on its true instances.

**Definition (`REPred`).** A predicate on natural numbers is recursively enumerable when membership is the existence of a numerical witness satisfying a recursively decidable relation.

**Definition (`REPred₂`).** A binary predicate is recursively enumerable when its pullback along the fixed pairing of natural numbers is recursively enumerable.

**Definition (`RecDecidable₂`).** A binary predicate is recursively decidable when its pullback along the fixed pairing of natural numbers is recursively decidable.

**Definition (`RecDecidable₃`).** A ternary predicate is recursively decidable when the predicate obtained by nested pairing is recursively decidable.

**Definition (`encodeList`).** The empty list is coded by zero, and a nonempty list is coded by pairing its head with the code of its tail and then taking the successor.

**Definition (`foldStep`).** A coded fold step either leaves a terminal state fixed or removes the next coded list entry and updates the accumulator with the given step operation.

**Definition (`foldCode`).** The fold of a coded list iterates the coded fold step for a bounded number of stages and returns the resulting accumulator.

**Definition (`halfParity`).** This primitive recursion simultaneously records the quotient and remainder of a natural number on division by two.

**Definition (`isOne`).** This zero-one characteristic function has value one exactly at the natural number one.

**Theorem (`isOne_eq_one_iff`).** The characteristic of one has value one exactly at the input one.

*Proof.* Expanding the two truncated differences shows that their sum vanishes exactly when the input is neither below nor above one. ∎

**Theorem (`isOne_one`).** The characteristic of one evaluates to one at one.

*Proof.* Substituting one makes both truncated differences zero, leaving one. ∎

**Theorem (`isOne_zero`).** The characteristic of one evaluates to zero at zero.

*Proof.* Substituting zero makes the second truncated difference one, which is subtracted from one. ∎

**Definition (`allListStp`).** This fold step preserves a success flag precisely when the current coded list entry satisfies the given characteristic function.

**Definition (`allListChar`).** This characteristic function tests whether every entry of a coded list satisfies a given numerical predicate.

**Definition (`bExistsFn`).** This bounded search characteristic records whether some index below the bound satisfies the given numerical predicate.

**Definition (`bForallFn`).** This bounded search characteristic records whether every index below the bound satisfies the given numerical predicate.

**Definition (`existsListStp`).** This fold step records whether the current coded list entry or an earlier entry satisfies the given characteristic function.

**Definition (`existsListChar`).** This characteristic function tests whether some entry of a coded list satisfies a given numerical predicate.

**Definition (`isBinDigit`).** This characteristic function recognizes exactly the two binary digits zero and one.

**Definition (`allBinDigitsChar`).** This characteristic function tests whether every entry of a coded list is a binary digit.

**Definition (`isZero`).** This zero-one characteristic function has value one exactly at zero.

**Theorem (`le_pair_right`).** Each component of a paired natural number is at most the pair code.

*Proof.* Expanding the pairing formula leaves the second component plus nonnegative terms. ∎

**Definition (`listLenStp`).** This fold step increments the accumulated length by one.

**Definition (`listLenChar`).** This numerical function computes the length of the list represented by a code.

**Definition (`lowOr`).** This operation computes the lowest binary digit of the bitwise disjunction of two natural numbers.

**Definition (`lorStep`).** This state transition removes the lowest bits of two numbers while accumulating their bitwise disjunction.

**Definition (`mapPairStep`).** This operation adjoins the pair formed from a fixed parameter and a list entry to an accumulated list code.

**Definition (`mapPairStp`).** This packed fold step applies the operation that pairs every list entry with a fixed parameter.

**Definition (`mapPairCode`).** This operation codes the list obtained by pairing a fixed natural number with every entry of a coded list.

**Definition (`mulBit`).** This operation combines two zero-one values by multiplication, thereby representing conjunction.

**Definition (`decodeFuelOkCharBody`).** This one-stage characteristic interprets a tagged code using previously computed values for its recursive subcodes.

**Definition (`decodeFuelOkChar`).** This fuel-bounded characteristic evaluates well-formedness of coded expressions by recursion on the available fuel.

**Definition (`myLor`).** This operation computes binary disjunction by repeatedly consuming the low bits of its two arguments.

**Definition (`natEqChar`).** This zero-one characteristic function recognizes equality of two natural numbers.

**Theorem (`natEqChar_eq_one_iff`).** The natural-number equality characteristic has value one exactly when its two arguments are equal.

*Proof.* The two opposite truncated differences vanish simultaneously exactly when the arguments are equal. ∎

**Definition (`codeBound`).** This recursively defined bound dominates every code of a sufficiently short list of binary digits.

**Definition (`listEqStpNonzero`).** This comparison step consumes one entry from a nonempty remaining list code and preserves the equality flag exactly when the entries agree.

**Definition (`listEqStp`).** This comparison step handles the empty-list case and otherwise advances the coded list comparison.

**Definition (`listEqChar`).** This characteristic function tests equality of the two lists represented by given codes.

**Definition (`listEqStep`).** This unpacked comparison step applies one coded-list equality update to a state and a new entry.

**Definition (`nthStp`).** This fold step counts list positions and retains the entry at the requested index, using a prescribed default if none is found.

**Definition (`nthCode`).** This operation returns the requested entry of a coded list, or a prescribed default beyond its length.

**Definition (`appendListTabFn`).** This table entry function reads first from one coded list and then from a second, with the split determined by the first length.

**Definition (`decodeFuelOkCharLookup`).** This table-based evaluator performs one tagged well-formedness step using prior values stored in a coded table.

**Definition (`dropListTabFn`).** This table entry function reads a coded list after shifting its index by the number of entries to discard.

**Definition (`reForallF`).** This update consumes one witness code and preserves a success flag when the corresponding decidable relation holds.

**Definition (`reForallStp`).** This packed update advances the witness-list test for universal membership in a recursively enumerable predicate.

**Definition (`reForallChar`).** This characteristic checks a list of proposed witnesses against the corresponding entries of another coded list.

**Theorem (`sqrt_le_add`).** Every natural number is at most the square of its natural square root plus twice that square root.

*Proof.* The input is below the next square, whose expansion is the square-root square plus twice the square root plus one; natural-number discreteness removes the final one. ∎

**Theorem (`pair_unpair`).** Pairing the two components obtained by unpairing a natural number recovers that number.

*Proof.* The square-root bounds locate the number in its triangular pairing block; substituting the resulting quotient and offset reconstructs it. ∎

**Definition (`tabStep`).** This recursion step adjoins the next value of a finite numerical table to its code.

**Definition (`tabCode`).** This operation codes the finite table of values of a numerical function below a given bound.

**Definition (`appendListCode`).** This operation produces a code for the concatenation of two coded lists.

**Definition (`dropCode`).** This operation produces a code for the list obtained after deleting a prescribed initial segment.

**Definition (`fuelTableStep`).** This operation constructs the next finite table of fuel-bounded values from the preceding table.

**Definition (`fuelTable`).** This primitive recursion builds the finite table of all fuel-bounded values below a prescribed code bound.

**Definition (`takeListTabFn`).** This table entry function reads an entry of a coded list at the table index.

**Definition (`takeCode`).** This operation produces a code for the prescribed initial segment of a coded list.

**Theorem (`unpair_snd_le`).** The second component obtained by unpairing a natural number is at most that number.

*Proof.* The second component is bounded by its reconstructed pair, and pairing the two unpaired components returns the original number. ∎

**Definition (`decodeList`).** Zero decodes to the empty list, while a successor code decodes by unpairing its predecessor into the head and the code of the tail.

**Theorem (`primrec_id`).** The numerical function defining id is primitive recursive.

*Proof.* The identity is the first projection after pairing the input with zero, hence is primitive recursive. ∎

**Theorem (`rec_add`).** The displayed primitive recursion computes add on natural numbers.

*Proof.* Induction on the second argument matches zero with the initial value and each successor with one further increment. ∎

**Theorem (`rec_mul`).** The displayed primitive recursion computes mul on natural numbers.

*Proof.* Induction on the second argument turns the successor equation into one further addition, matching multiplication by a successor. ∎

**Theorem (`primrec_add`).** The numerical function defining add is primitive recursive.

*Proof.* Primitive recursion from the first input, adding one at each successor of the second, computes addition. ∎

**Theorem (`primrec_mul`).** The numerical function defining mul is primitive recursive.

*Proof.* The mul construction is assembled from primitive-recursive projections, constants, pairing, composition and its displayed bounded fold or primitive recursion. ∎

**Theorem (`rec_pred`).** The displayed primitive recursion computes pred on natural numbers.

*Proof.* The zero clause returns zero, while at a successor the recursion returns the preceding index. ∎

**Theorem (`primrec_pred`).** The numerical function defining pred is primitive recursive.

*Proof.* Primitive recursion returns the predecessor stage at each successor and zero at the base. ∎

**Theorem (`rec_sub`).** The displayed primitive recursion computes sub on natural numbers.

*Proof.* Induction on the second argument matches zero subtraction and one further predecessor at each successor. ∎

**Theorem (`primrec_sub`).** The numerical function defining sub is primitive recursive.

*Proof.* Primitive recursion from the first input applies predecessor once for each unit of the second input. ∎

**Theorem (`nat_mul_eq_one`).** A product of natural numbers is one exactly when both factors are one.

*Proof.* Unique factorization of one in the natural numbers forces each factor to be one, and the converse is immediate by multiplication. ∎

**Theorem (`primrec_add₂`).** The numerical function defining add₂ is primitive recursive.

*Proof.* The add₂ construction is assembled from primitive-recursive projections, constants, pairing, composition and its displayed bounded fold or primitive recursion. ∎

