**Theorem (`bForallFn_le_one`).** The bounded universal characteristic is always at most one.

*Proof.* Induction on the bound starts at one and each new selector returns either zero or a one-characteristic, both at most one. ∎

**Theorem (`bForallFn_eq_one_iff`).** The bounded universal characteristic is one exactly when the tested function is one at every index below the bound.

*Proof.* Induction on the bound separates the previous indices from the new final index; the binary accumulator remains one precisely when both parts succeed. ∎

**Theorem (`bExistsFn_le_one`).** The bounded existential characteristic is always at most one.

*Proof.* Induction on the bound observes that an already successful search stays at one, while an unsuccessful search takes the next one-characteristic, which is binary. ∎

**Theorem (`bExistsFn_congr`).** Two bounded existential searches agree when their tested values agree at every index below the bound.

*Proof.* Induction on the bound uses agreement on all earlier indices for the recursive value and agreement at the new final index for the next update. ∎

**Theorem (`bExistsFn_eq_one_iff`).** The bounded existential characteristic is one exactly when some index below the bound has tested value one.

*Proof.* Induction on the bound splits a witness below the previous bound from the newly added final index and follows the two selector cases. ∎

